Source paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/ba_computer_science_past_papers/response/2577745/attach/2/202402%20183%20CS%20Papers.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[released examination archive], member `2014/T/a13757w1.pdf`. The original cover dates the examination to **24 June 2015**, 9.30am to 11.00am. The folder name is not the examination year.

**Incomplete source; partial solutions.** The held PDF has an overwritten block of 65,536 bytes. Its cover is readable, but the right-hand portions of printed page 2 and the left-hand portions of printed page 3 are missing. The available TeX transcribes only the cover. The numbering below follows the surviving labels on printed page 2. Where a mathematical argument can be supplied, its assumptions are stated explicitly; those assumptions do not reconstruct unreadable source text. No leaf is certified as a complete answer to the original paper. Printed page 3 also contains readable fragments concerning <simplicial complexes>, <topological manifolds>, <topological surfaces> and a <polygonal schema>, but its question labels and the beginnings of the requests cannot be recovered. Its subdivision is therefore not invented here.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

The <connectedness> definitions needed for the following arguments are these. A <topological space> $X$ is a <connected space> when it cannot be written as $X=U\cup V$ for two disjoint, nonempty <open sets> $U,V$. Such a pair would be a <separation of a topological space>. Equivalently, the only <subsets> of $X$ that are both <open> and <closed> are $\varnothing$ and $X$: a nontrivial such <subset> and its <complement> give a <separation of a topological space>, and either member of a <separation of a topological space> is also <closed>.

A <subset> $A\subseteq X$ is a <connected subset> when it is a <connected space> in its <subspace topology>. Thus it is the relative <open sets> $A\cap U$, rather than just <open sets> of $X$ contained in $A$, that must be tested. In particular, the <empty set> and every <singleton> are <connected subsets>.
$$
\boxed{A\text{ is connected}\iff A\text{ has no separation in its subspace topology}.}
$$
The missing right-hand text prevents checking whether these definitions exhaust this leaf.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

A sufficient hypothesis for the surviving <connectedness> conclusion is that $A,B$ are <connected subsets> of the same <topological space> and $A\cap B\ne\varnothing$. Here is the full proof under that hypothesis; the original overlap hypothesis lies in the missing portion of the scan.

Suppose $U,V$ form a <separation of a topological space> of $A\cup B$. Intersecting with $A$ gives disjoint relative <open sets> covering $A$. By <connectedness>, $A$ lies entirely in $U$ or entirely in $V$. The same argument applies to $B$. A point of $A\cap B$ forces both <subsets> to lie on the same side. The other side is empty, a contradiction.
$$
\boxed{A,B\text{ connected},\ A\cap B\ne\varnothing\ \Longrightarrow\ A\cup B\text{ connected}.}
$$
An overlap condition cannot simply be omitted: $\{0\}$ and $\{1\}$ are <connected subsets> of the <real line>, while their <union> has the <discrete topology> and is not a <connected space>.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

The visible data include a <connected subset> $A\subseteq X$ and a <function> beginning $f:X\to\cdots$. Its target, further hypotheses and conclusion are missing. The following establishes the standard image result with its necessary <continuity> hypothesis explicitly supplied; it is not a reconstruction of the lost request.

If $f:X\to Y$ is a <continuous map>, then its restriction $f|_A:A\to f(A)$ is a <continuous map> for the <subspace topologies>. Were $U,V$ a <separation of a topological space> of $f(A)$, their <inverse images> under this restriction would be disjoint, nonempty relative <open sets> covering $A$. Nonemptiness follows because this restriction is a <surjection> onto $f(A)$. This contradicts the <connectedness> of $A$.
$$
\boxed{f\text{ continuous},\ A\text{ connected}\ \Longrightarrow\ f(A)\text{ connected}.}
$$
Without <continuity> the assertion fails: the <function> on $\mathbb R$ taking value $0$ for $x<0$ and $1$ for $x\geq0$ maps the <connected> <real line> onto the disconnected two-point <topological subspace> $\{0,1\}$.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

For <topological spaces> $(X,\mathcal T_X)$ and $(Y,\mathcal T_Y)$, use all <open> rectangles:
$$
\mathcal B=\{U\times V:U\in\mathcal T_X,\ V\in\mathcal T_Y\}.
$$
To verify the <basis of a topology> conditions, first note that $X\times Y\in\mathcal B$, so every point is covered. Next, whenever a point lies in two members of $\mathcal B$, their <intersection> is itself a member containing that point:
$$
(U_1\times V_1)\cap(U_2\times V_2)
=(U_1\cap U_2)\times(V_1\cap V_2).
$$
Both coordinate <intersections> are <open sets>. Thus arbitrary <unions> of members of $\mathcal B$ form a <topology>: they include the <empty set> and the whole <Cartesian product>, arbitrary <unions> remain of this form, and finite <intersections> distribute into <unions> of the displayed rectangles. This is the <product topology>.
$$
\boxed{\mathcal B=\{U\times V:U\in\mathcal T_X,\ V\in\mathcal T_Y\}\text{ is a basis for the product topology}.}
$$
The scan loses the ends of the request's lines, so its exact full wording remains unverified.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Hausdorff property of a nonempty product> follows directly from <open> rectangles. Suppose first that $X,Y$ are <Hausdorff spaces>. Distinct points $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)$ differ in at least one coordinate. If $x_1\ne x_2$, choose disjoint <open> <neighbourhoods> $U_1,U_2$ in $X$. Then $U_1\times Y$ and $U_2\times Y$ are disjoint <open> <neighbourhoods> in the <product topology>. If instead $y_1\ne y_2$, use $X\times V_1$ and $X\times V_2$ from disjoint <open> <neighbourhoods> in $Y$.

Conversely, suppose $X\times Y$ is <Hausdorff> and choose $y_0\in Y$. For distinct $x_1,x_2\in X$, take disjoint <open> <neighbourhoods> $O_1,O_2$ of $(x_1,y_0),(x_2,y_0)$. The <basis of a topology> from part (i) gives rectangles
$$
(x_i,y_0)\in U_i\times V_i\subseteq O_i\qquad(i=1,2).
$$
Since $y_0\in V_1\cap V_2$, any point $x\in U_1\cap U_2$ would put $(x,y_0)$ in both rectangles, contradicting their disjointness. Therefore $U_1,U_2$ are disjoint <open> <neighbourhoods> of $x_1,x_2$, proving the <Hausdorff condition> for $X$. Fixing $x_0\in X$ proves it for $Y$ in exactly the same way.
$$
\boxed{X,Y\ne\varnothing\quad\Longrightarrow\quad X\times Y\text{ Hausdorff}\iff X,Y\text{ Hausdorff}.}
$$
The nonempty-factor assumption is visible in the surviving introduction. The right-hand end of this leaf is missing, so no further source requirement can be ruled out.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

The precise definitions and hypotheses on $A,B$ are missing. Under the explicit sufficient assumptions that $X,Y$ are nonempty <connected spaces> and $A\subsetneq X$, $B\subsetneq Y$, the <connected complement of a product of proper subsets> has the following proof.

Choose $x_0\in X\setminus A$ and $y_0\in Y\setminus B$. Inside $C=(X\times Y)\setminus(A\times B)$ consider
$$
H=(\{x_0\}\times Y)\cup(X\times\{y_0\}).
$$
The vertical and horizontal <topological subspaces> are <homeomorphic> to $Y$ and $X$, respectively: the coordinate projections and the maps inserting a fixed coordinate are mutually inverse <continuous maps>. They are therefore <connected subsets>, and they meet at $(x_0,y_0)$. Part (a)(ii) makes $H$ a <connected subset>.

Every $(x,y)\in C$ has $x\notin A$ or $y\notin B$. In the first case the whole <connected> vertical copy $\{x\}\times Y$ lies in $C$ and meets $H$ at $(x,y_0)$. In the second case the <connected> horizontal copy $X\times\{y\}$ lies in $C$ and meets $H$ at $(x_0,y)$. These copies cover $C$ and each meets the same <connected> $H$.

For completeness, a <separation of a topological space> of $C$ would put $H$ on one side by <connectedness>; every one of the attached <connected subsets> must then lie on that side too, since it meets $H$. They cover $C$, so the other side would be empty. This is the <connected union with a connected hub> argument.
$$
\boxed{X,Y\text{ connected},\ A\subsetneq X,\ B\subsetneq Y\ \Longrightarrow\ (X\times Y)\setminus(A\times B)\text{ connected}.}
$$
This conditional result does not settle which hypotheses were actually printed in the missing portion.

==== iv
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $X$ be an <infinite set> with its <cofinite topology>. For any <subset> $S\subseteq X$, its <subspace topology> is again the <cofinite topology>, now on $S$. Indeed, intersecting $S$ with $X\setminus F$ for finite $F\subseteq X$ gives $S\setminus(F\cap S)$; conversely, every finite exclusion from $S$ is obtained by taking that same finite exclusion from $X$.

If $S$ is infinite, any two nonempty relative <open sets> have finite <complements> in $S$ and must intersect. Thus $S$ is a <hyperconnected space>, hence a <connected space>. If $S$ is finite, its <cofinite topology> is the <discrete topology>. For $|S|\geq2$, the two <open sets> $\{s\}$ and $S\setminus\{s\}$ give a <separation of a topological space>. The <empty set> and <singletons> are <connected>. This proves the classification in <connected subsets of a cofinite space>:
$$
\boxed{S\text{ connected}\iff |S|\leq1\text{ or }S\text{ is infinite}.}
$$
The <Cartesian product> nevertheless contains disconnected <subsets>. Choose distinct $a,b\in X$ and put $D=\{(a,a),(b,b)\}$. The <open> rectangle $(X\setminus\{b\})\times X$ has trace $\{(a,a)\}$ on $D$, while $(X\setminus\{a\})\times X$ has trace $\{(b,b)\}$. These two traces are disjoint, nonempty relative <open sets> covering $D$.
$$
\boxed{D=\{(a,a),(b,b)\}\text{ is a disconnected subspace of }X\times X\quad(a\ne b).}
$$
The rectangle <open sets> used to separate these two points need not be disjoint in the whole <Cartesian product>; only their traces on $D$ must be disjoint. This distinction explains why the factors can have such strong <connectedness> properties while this small <topological subspace> does not. The ends of the source lines remain missing.

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

The <compactness> definition used in the next proof is as follows. A <topological space> $X$ is a <compact space> when every <open cover> of $X$ has a <finite subcover>. More explicitly, whenever <open sets> $(U_i)_{i\in I}$ satisfy $X=\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i$, finitely many indices $i_1,\ldots,i_n$ suffice to give $X=U_{i_1}\cup\cdots\cup U_{i_n}$.

A <subset> is a <compact subset> if its <subspace topology> is <compact>. Equivalently, every covering of that <subset> by <open sets> of the ambient <topological space> has a <finite subcover>, because relative <open sets> are intersections with ambient <open sets>.
$$
\boxed{X\text{ compact}\iff\text{every open cover of }X\text{ has a finite subcover}.}
$$
The defining term in this leaf itself is cut off in the scan. This records the definition needed for the visible compact-image argument, without claiming to recover the missing source wording.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

For the standard <continuous image of a compact space> argument, suppose explicitly that $X$ is <compact> and $f:X\to Y$ is a <continuous map>. Give $f(X)$ its <subspace topology>, and let $(V_i)_{i\in I}$ be any <open cover> of it. The restriction $X\to f(X)$ is a <continuous map>, so the <inverse images> $f^{-1}(V_i)$ are <open sets> in $X$ and cover $X$. By <compactness>, finitely many cover $X$. Applying $f$ shows that the corresponding finitely many $V_i$ cover $f(X)$:
$$
X=\bigcup_{j=1}^n f^{-1}(V_{i_j})
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
f(X)=\bigcup_{j=1}^n V_{i_j}.
$$
This proves
$$
\boxed{X\text{ compact},\ f\text{ continuous}\ \Longrightarrow\ f(X)\text{ compact}.}
$$
No <Hausdorff condition> on $Y$ is needed. The source loses the right-hand text containing the rest of the assumptions on $f$, so <continuity> has been supplied explicitly here rather than silently inferred from <compactness> of $X$.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

For the <canonical projection of a quotient set> $p:X\to X/\mathcal R$, put
$$
\mathcal T_q=\{U\subseteq X/\mathcal R:p^{-1}(U)\in\mathcal T\}.
$$
This is the <quotient topology>. It is a <topology> because <inverse images> take the <empty set> and whole target to the <empty set> and $X$, commute with arbitrary <unions>, and commute with finite <intersections>. Thus the corresponding axioms follow from those for $\mathcal T$.

The <function> $p$ is <surjective>, since every <equivalence class> has a representative. It is <continuous> because the full <inverse image> of every <open set> of $\mathcal T_q$ is <open> by definition. In fact, $\mathcal T_q$ is the finest <topology> making $p$ a <continuous map>: any such <topology> can contain only <subsets> with <open> <inverse images>.
$$
\boxed{U\text{ open in }X/\mathcal R\iff p^{-1}(U)\text{ open in }X.}
$$
The scan preserves the request for the definition and the start of a further proof request, but the latter's conclusion is missing. The verifications above do not certify that all of it has been recovered.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

The <universal property of the quotient topology> can be proved using exactly the defining <open set> test. For a <function> $g:X/\mathcal R\to Z$, if $g$ is <continuous>, then $g\circ p$ is <continuous> by <function composition> of <continuous maps>.

Conversely, suppose $g\circ p$ is <continuous>. For any <open set> $V\subseteq Z$,
$$
p^{-1}(g^{-1}(V))=(g\circ p)^{-1}(V)
$$
is <open> in $X$. The <quotient topology> therefore says that $g^{-1}(V)$ is <open> in $X/\mathcal R$. This holds for every <open set> $V$, which is precisely the <continuity> of $g$.
$$
\boxed{g:X/\mathcal R\to Z\text{ continuous}\iff g\circ p:X\to Z\text{ continuous}.}
$$
The same argument explains how to construct such a <function>: a <function> $f:X\to Z$ induces a unique <function> $g$ with $g\circ p=f$ exactly when $f$ is constant on every <equivalence class>. Its formula is $g([x])=f(x)$; the preceding equivalence then proves its <continuity> whenever $f$ is <continuous>. The right-hand ends of the source lines are absent, so this remains an answer to the identifiable mathematical content rather than a certification of the full printed leaf.

=== c
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

For <collapsing a subset to a point>, write $q:X\to X_A$ for the <quotient map>, with $A$ as the collapsed <equivalence class> and each point outside $A$ its own <equivalence class>. The crucial <saturation of a subset> calculation, valid for any <subset> $K$, is
$$
q^{-1}(q(K))=
\begin{cases}
K,&K\cap A=\varnothing,\\
K\cup A,&K\cap A\ne\varnothing.
\end{cases}
$$
If $K$ misses $A$, no new points are added: every <equivalence class> meeting $K$ is a <singleton>. If $K$ meets $A$, the whole <equivalence class> $A$ is added and no other points are added.

The <closed set> version of the <quotient topology> test gives
$$
\boxed{q(K)\text{ closed in }X_A\iff q^{-1}(q(K))\text{ closed in }X.}
$$
Indeed, take <complements> in the <open set> test, using $q^{-1}(X_A\setminus q(K))=X\setminus q^{-1}(q(K))$. In particular, if $K$ is <closed> and $K\cap A=\varnothing$, then $q^{-1}(q(K))=K$ is <closed>, so $q(K)$ is <closed>. If $K$ meets $A$, the correct test is instead whether $K\cup A$ is <closed>.

The scan loses the value after $K\cap A=$ and most of the requested conclusion. The displayed criterion handles both possibilities without guessing the missing hypothesis.

